## Peer Response 1

You make a good point regarding Land and Aronson’s argument about technology being biased towards inequality. A National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) study found higher rates of false positives for Asian and African American faces relative to images of Caucasians, with some recognition algorithms showing error rates 10 to 100 times higher. This is not just a glitch, and your suggestion for regular audits is important. However, NIST also argues that performance varies significantly across algorithms and that some systems can be both more accurate and more equitable (NIST, 2019).

Expanding on your point about mitigation: regular audit testing, experimentation, and evaluation build a foundation for trusting the implementation. Monitoring should be designed to detect potential issues early and improve the cycle over time. In addition, keeping a digital record of decisions can improve traceability and accountability (Captive Insurance Times, 2025).

