## Summary Post (Unit 3)

In my initial post, I explained the risks to fairness and accountability linked to the use of data and technology in human rights investigations. I identified two main issues. First, datasets can be biased, especially against minorities and vulnerable groups, which can reinforce existing inequalities and lead to unfair outcomes (Vicente and Matute, 2023). Second, technology can make accountability more difficult when mistakes happen, especially when AI systems work as “black boxes” and their decisions are not transparent (Land and Aronson, 2020; Chikwarti and Wong, 2025).

In the peer replies, both responses agreed that biased datasets are a serious concern. They developed the discussion further by clarifying which measures could reduce these risks. One peer highlighted the importance of Algorithmic Impact Assessments, representative sampling, and Explainable AI frameworks. These measures are useful because they can identify harmful effects before a system is deployed and make decisions easier to understand and challenge (Metcalf et al., 2021; Buolamwini and Gebru, 2018). Another peer reinforced the issue of bias by referencing NIST’s findings on facial recognition errors across Asian and African American groups, showing that some systems produce unfair results depending on the population being analysed (NIST, 2019). The peer responses also emphasised regular audits, testing, and monitoring to detect issues early and improve accountability (Captive Insurance Times, 2025).

Overall, this discussion helped me reflect more critically on the topic. My initial post focused mainly on biased datasets and lack of accountability, but the peer replies showed more practical ways to reduce these risks during the design phase. This discussion confirmed that technology is not neutral in human rights investigations. To protect fairness and accountability, systems must include community input, transparency, regular evaluation, and human oversight from the start.

